Subject: Clear native AI variant data before narrow scalar stores
Upstream-base: f75ccf1ad20e5bd7585962990db08067c0ae498a

Variant's typed constructor stores a scalar through a cast of its data union,
writing only sizeof(T) bytes. CopyFrom then copies all 12 union bytes, and
ScriptState::Update runs a state's Update script only while the copied
DesireUpdate's mData.pointer is zero. On the Wii the pointer member is 4 bytes
and coincides with the int; natively it spans 8 bytes, so that test also reads
4 bytes the narrow store left from the stack. The Update script was then
skipped or run depending on stack contents: AI behaviour drifted from the
original and two LAN machines lost lockstep within seconds.

On native builds the constructor now clears the union before the store, so a
pointer-width read of an int result sees the int with zero upper bytes, as on
the console. Wii builds are unchanged.

diff --git a/include/Game/AI/Variant.h b/include/Game/AI/Variant.h
index c4e73ee..fb9c332 100644
--- a/include/Game/AI/Variant.h
+++ b/include/Game/AI/Variant.h
@@ -2,6 +2,9 @@
 #define _VARIANT_H_
 
 #include <stddef.h>
+#if defined(MSCHARGED_NATIVE)
+#include <string.h>
+#endif
 
 #include "NL/nlBasicString.h"
 #include "NL/nlMath.h"
@@ -50,6 +53,12 @@ public:
     template <typename T>
     Variant(eVariantType type, T value)
     {
+#if defined(MSCHARGED_NATIVE)
+        // A native pointer spans 8 bytes, more than the 4-byte scalars: clear
+        // the union so CopyFrom's 12-byte copy and pointer-width zero tests
+        // never read bytes this narrow store left from the stack.
+        memset(&mData, 0, sizeof(mData));
+#endif
         *(T*)&mData = value;
         mType = type;
     }
